  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 210 PM MDT TUE OCT 6 2026\nOne final day of warm and dry conditions is expected Friday before a \nmajor pattern change Saturday through Tuesday. An upper-level trough \nwill move south from British Columbia, increasing wind speeds in \nhigher terrain as it moves closer to the area. A cold front will \nmove across the area on Saturday, cooling high temperatures by about \n20 degrees areawide from Friday. Breezy winds will expand to lower \nelevations as the front moves through. \n\nThe trough is favored to close off and dive south through the \nPacific Northwest, moving into SE Oregon and SW Idaho Saturday into \nSunday. Once late Sunday into Monday rolls around, models are split \non having the system move even further south into CA/NV or staying \nprogressive and moving east. These range of solutions greatly \nincrease uncertainty early next week, as ensembles have consistently \ndeveloped significant precipitation to the east of the low center \nwhere better moisture will exist. The current forecast is a 40-60% \nchance of precipitation across central Idaho Saturday night through \nTuesday, with a 10-30% chance elsewhere. Temperatures are expected \nto decrease to 5 to 10 degrees below normal Sunday through Tuesday, \nwhich will also take many higher elevations to below freezing during \novernight periods. Temperatures low enough for frost at lower \nelevations may also occur, but confidence in that is low currently.\nCooler air will also mean lower snow levels, allowing for snow \non the higher peaks. However, similar to the precipitation \nforecast, how much cooler depends on the exact track of the low.\nThe most aggressive model guidance has snow levels in SE Oregon\ngetting near 4000 feet at the lowest. Winds will remain breezy \nthrough Sunday, then start to subside early next week.",
